I,ve reviewed the new architecture proposal (056e005) and the subsequent architecture audit/review (5f79ba4), rather than just the earlier repository audit.

My recommendation is do not implement the proposal literally. The architectural direction is good, but the proposal mixes three different concerns—configuration, machine state, and synchronization mechanics—and that will become painful as soon as you have more than a couple of machines.

Executive verdict

Proposal: 6/10
Architecture review: 8.5/10
Recommended target architecture: GitOps Configuration Reconciliation System

The key change I'd make is:

> Git should describe desired configuration. The machine should own runtime state. A reconciler should continuously converge the machine toward Git's desired state.



Not:

> Machine state → automatically git add . → commit → push → becomes configuration.



That distinction is the foundation of the whole system.


---

1. What I would KEEP

The proposal has several excellent architectural decisions.

✅ Git as source of truth

Keep this.

✅ GNU Stow on Linux

Keep Stow as the filesystem projection layer.

✅ Host-specific configuration

Absolutely keep this concept.

✅ PR → CI → main

Keep this as the promotion path.

✅ Native OS integration

Linux:

systemd
udev

Windows:

PowerShell
Task Scheduler

Good choices.

✅ Automated bootstrap

Definitely keep the goal.

✅ Event-driven synchronization

Keep the concept, but change the trigger architecture.

The proposal currently tries to use power/battery events as Git synchronization triggers.

That's too tightly coupled.


---

2. The biggest architectural change

The proposal currently treats host branches as the configuration hierarchy:

main
 ├── host-linux-laptop
 └── host-win-desktop

I would not make branches the configuration model.

Instead:

GitHub
                        │
                      main
                        │
                 Desired State
                        │
             ┌──────────┴──────────┐
             │                     │
        Linux Host              Windows Host
             │                     │
       Host Profile            Host Profile
             │                     │
             └──────────┬──────────┘
                        │
                   Reconciler
                        │
                     System

And inside Git:

dotfiles/
├── config/
│   ├── common/
│   ├── linux/
│   ├── windows/
│   └── wsl/
│
├── hosts/
│   ├── linux-laptop/
│   ├── windows-desktop/
│   └── wsl-dev/
│
├── modules/
│   ├── shell/
│   ├── git/
│   ├── terminal/
│   ├── editor/
│   └── desktop/
│
├── manifests/
├── scripts/
├── tests/
├── policy/
└── .github/

Branches become workflow mechanics, not configuration composition.

That's an important distinction.


---

3. Introduce an explicit machine manifest

This is the missing abstraction.

Something like:

# hosts/linux-laptop/host.yaml

id: linux-laptop

platform: linux
environment: native

profile:
  - common
  - linux
  - laptop

packages:
  - zsh
  - git
  - tmux
  - ripgrep
  - stow

modules:
  - shell
  - git
  - terminal

For WSL:

id: wsl-dev

platform: windows
environment: wsl

profile:
  - common
  - linux
  - wsl

This solves a major problem identified in the architecture review: uname cannot distinguish native Linux from WSL.

Your system should explicitly model:

PLATFORM
    linux
    windows

ENVIRONMENT
    native
    wsl
    vm
    container

That's much more future-proof.


---

4. Don't let git add . become your state capture mechanism

This is probably my #1 implementation rule.

The proposal says:

git add .
git commit -m "Automated state checkpoint..."
git push

The architecture review correctly identifies this as dangerous.

Instead:

Machine
   │
   ▼
DISCOVER
   │
   ▼
FILTER
   │
   ▼
NORMALIZE
   │
   ▼
PLAN
   │
   ▼
DIFF
   │
   ▼
APPLY
   │
   ▼
VERIFY

Only explicitly declared configuration gets captured.

Think allow-list, not ignore-list.


---

5. Separate three types of state

I strongly recommend formalizing this.

Desired state

Lives in Git.

.git/
config/
hosts/
modules/
manifests/

Runtime state

Lives on the machine.

~/.cache/
~/.local/state/
systemd runtime state
Windows Task Scheduler state
package manager state

Observed state

Generated by the reconciler.

state/
    inventory.json
    drift.json
    last-sync.json

Observed state should not automatically become desired state.

That gives you:

Desired State
      ↓
  Reconciler
      ↓
Observed State
      ↓
     Drift

rather than:

Observed State
      ↓
    git add .
      ↓
Desired State


---

6. Replace "Git sync" with a reconciler

This is where I think your project can become significantly more interesting.

Instead of:

git-sync-host

make the conceptual core:

dotctl

or:

dotfilesctl

with operations such as:

dotctl discover
dotctl plan
dotctl apply
dotctl verify
dotctl status
dotctl drift
dotctl sync

Then:

dotctl reconcile

becomes the central operation.

The OS-specific automation simply invokes it.

Linux:

systemd
   ↓
dotctl reconcile

Windows:

Task Scheduler
   ↓
dotctl reconcile

Manual:

dotctl reconcile

CI:

dotctl test

That is dramatically cleaner.


---

7. Battery events should NOT be the primary synchronization model

The architecture review correctly flags this.

I'd use triggers like:

LOGIN
BOOT
NETWORK AVAILABLE
TIMER
MANUAL
GIT CHANGE
POWER EVENT

But all of them eventually call:

reconcile

Use a timer as the safety net.

For example:

systemd timer
     │
     └── every 15–30 min
              │
              ▼
         dotctl reconcile

Then event-driven triggers can accelerate reconciliation.

This gives you:

event-driven + eventually consistent

instead of:

event-dependent.


---

8. Make reconciliation idempotent

This needs to become a hard architectural invariant.

Every operation should satisfy:

apply(state)
apply(state)
apply(state)
apply(state)

→ same final state.

Your installer should therefore become:

bootstrap
    ↓
discover
    ↓
ensure dependencies
    ↓
ensure identity
    ↓
ensure repository
    ↓
ensure profile
    ↓
ensure symlinks
    ↓
ensure services
    ↓
verify

Not:

bootstrap
    ↓
create
    ↓
push
    ↓
hope

The architecture review correctly calls this out as one of the largest gaps.


---

9. Git branches: modify the proposal

I wouldn't completely throw away host branches.

I'd use:

main
 │
 ├── feature/*
 └── host/*

But host branches should represent host configuration changes, not continuously changing runtime state.

For example:

host/linux-laptop

can contain:

hosts/linux-laptop/

But the machine should not continuously mutate the branch every time something changes.

Instead:

Machine discovers drift
        ↓
dotctl drift
        ↓
human/agent reviews
        ↓
explicit capture
        ↓
PR
        ↓
main

This keeps Git history meaningful.


---

10. Security architecture

The proposal's hook-based protection needs to be downgraded from security control to developer guardrail.

The actual hierarchy should be:

GitHub Ruleset
       ↓
PR required
       ↓
CI required
       ↓
review
       ↓
merge

Local hooks:

developer convenience

not:

security boundary

The architecture review gets this exactly right.

Also make the security policy executable:

secret scan
PII scan
forbidden paths
machine-path scan
workflow permissions
dependency policy
shell security


---

11. CI should become a first-class test system

Don't simply test:

./bootstrap.sh

You want test categories:

Static

ShellCheck
bash -n
zsh -n
YAML
JSON
JSONC
TOML

Policy

secret detection
PII detection
forbidden paths
absolute machine paths
permissions
repository references

Stow

every package
every supported profile

Integration

fresh HOME
fresh repository
bootstrap
stow
verify

Idempotency

This is critical:

bootstrap #1
bootstrap #2
bootstrap #3

Expected:

0 unintended changes

Drift

apply
mutate configuration
detect drift
reconcile
verify

That is much closer to real GitOps.


